# ex-vectors.golden --- EX (MENU), Read-request frames
#
# SOURCE MANUAL
#   Title (cover, PDF page 1):  FT-891  CAT Operation Reference Book
#   Publisher:                  YAESU MUSEN CO., LTD.  (Copyright 2019)
#   Printed revision:           1909-C   (printed bottom-right of the back cover,
#                               PDF page 20; the cover carries no revision)
#   Chart used:                 EX command block, printed folio 7, PDF page 8
#   Menu chart used:            the P1/Function/P2/Digits table that follows the EX
#                               block --- printed folios 7, 8 and 9
#                               (PDF pages 8, 9 and 10). It begins at row "0101
#                               AGC FAST DELAY" on printed folio 7 and ends at row
#                               "1803 LCD VERSION" on printed folio 9.
#   Command-list entry:         printed folio 3, PDF page 4
#   Derivation date:            04/09/2026
#
# METHOD
#   Located at 300 dpi; the EX block re-rendered at 600 dpi (pdftoppm -r 600) and
#   cropped in a left and a right strip with magick, because the block's rows run
#   past the ten-column grid into "~ / n-1 / n" cells that are not legible at
#   300 dpi. Two independent counts of the Read chart (left-to-right, then by
#   per-parameter cell totals) both gave 7. The Digits column was then counted a
#   second, independent way: the column was cropped out of each of the three menu
#   chart pages and rotated 90 degrees so the whole column could be read in one
#   pass; that pass agreed with the row-by-row reading -- maximum 4 on printed
#   folio 7, maximum 5 on printed folio 8, maximum 4 on printed folio 9.
#
#   NO code, no generator, no other file and no other document was consulted.
#
# WHICH CHART THE LAYOUT WAS COUNTED FROM, AND WHY
#   Counted from the EX *Read* chart, because every vector below is a
#   Read-direction request. The EX Set and Answer charts print a different,
#   variable-length layout (they carry P2 as well) and are recorded below as
#   comments only.
#
# COUNTED FRAME LENGTH: 7 bytes (terminator included) --- fixed, for every menu
# item, because a Read request carries no P2.
#
# POSITION-BY-POSITION FIELD MAP (EX Read chart, counted)
#   pos 1      "E"    command character (printed literal)
#   pos 2      "X"    command character (printed literal)
#   pos 3      P1     legend verbatim: "P1  : 0101 - 1803 (MENU Number)"
#   pos 4      P1     "
#   pos 5      P1     "
#   pos 6      P1     "
#   pos 7      ";"    terminator (printed literal)
#   pos 8-10   ---    printed as empty cells
#   (P1 is thus 4 positions, counted, not inferred; the legend's "0101 - 1803"
#   range happens to agree, but the width used here is the counted cell count.)
#
#   The second legend entry, verbatim: "P2  : Parameter (See Table below)"
#
# THE SET AND ANSWER CHARTS, AS PRINTED (comment only --- no vector below uses them)
#   Set:     pos 1 "E"  pos 2 "X"  pos 3-6 "P1"  pos 7 "P2"  ... "~" ... "n-1" "P2"  "n" ";"
#   Answer:  pos 1 "E"  pos 2 "X"  pos 3-6 "P1"  pos 7 "P2"  ... "~" ... "n-1" "P2"  "n" ";"
#   Both are printed with the literal cells "~", "n-1" and "n" in the position
#   header, i.e. the manual leaves the total length n open and defers it to the
#   menu chart's "Digits" column: n = 6 + Digits + 1.
#
# ANSWER FRAME SHAPE FOR ONE ITEM, PARAMETER POSITIONS MARKED, NO VALUE INVENTED
#   Item 0803 "OTHER DISP", whose Digits column prints 5:
#     pos  1     "E"
#     pos  2     "X"
#     pos  3     "0"   \
#     pos  4     "8"    |  P1 = the menu number, 4 counted positions
#     pos  5     "0"    |
#     pos  6     "3"   /
#     pos  7     P2[1]  \
#     pos  8     P2[2]   |
#     pos  9     P2[3]   |  P2 = 5 positions, from the Digits column value 5
#     pos 10     P2[4]   |  (legend for the value: "-3000 Hz - 0 - +3000 Hz
#     pos 11     P2[5]  /    (P2= -3000 - -0000 or +0000 - +3000) (10 Hz/steps)")
#     pos 12     ";"
#   Total 12 bytes = 6 + 5 + 1. No P2 value is written here.
#
# MENU ITEMS CHOSEN, AND WHY
#   0101  AGC FAST DELAY   --- the FIRST row of the FIRST group (01xx) on the chart,
#                              printed folio 7. P2 "20 - 4000 (msec) (P2= 0020 -
#                              4000, 20 msec/step)", Digits 4.
#   0803  OTHER DISP       --- the item whose Digits column prints the LARGEST value
#                              found anywhere in the chart: Digits = 5, on printed
#                              folio 8. Only one other row shares it, 0804 OTHER
#                              SHIFT, also Digits 5; every other row in the whole
#                              chart prints 1, 2, 3 or 4.
#   0506  CAT RATE         --- free choice. P2 "0: 4800 bps  1: 9600 bps
#                              2: 19200 bps  3: 38400 bps", Digits 1, printed folio 7.
#   1803  LCD VERSION      --- the LAST row of the LAST group (18xx) on the chart,
#                              printed folio 9. P2 "0000 - 9999 (V01-23 = 0123)",
#                              Digits 4.
#
# BYTES THE MANUAL DETERMINES
#   All seven. pos 1-2 are the printed command characters, pos 3-6 are the menu
#   numbers printed in the chart's P1 column, pos 7 is the printed terminator.
#
# INHERITED-ASSUMED BYTES
#   NONE in the four vectors below --- a Read request contains no value the manual
#   leaves open.
#   (For the record, the Answer *shape* above does rest on one thing the manual
#   does not itself state: that the "n" of the Set/Answer charts equals
#   6 + Digits + 1, i.e. that the Digits column gives the P2 width in the frame.
#   That reading is INHERITED-ASSUMED. The chart itself contradicts it in at
#   least one row -- see provenance.md, item 0905.)
#
# DIRECTION SUPPORT: the command list (printed folio 3, PDF page 4) prints
#   Command "EX" | Function "MENU" | Set "O" | Read "O" | Ans. "O" | AI "O",
#   and the EX detail block agrees: all three charts are printed and filled.
#
# HARDWARE STATUS: UNVERIFIED for every vector below.

ex_read_0101_agc_fast_delay_first_group	EX0101;
ex_read_0506_cat_rate	EX0506;
ex_read_0803_other_disp_largest_digits_5	EX0803;
ex_read_1803_lcd_version_last_group	EX1803;
